-- Database access controls (security hardening, 2026-10-12).
--
-- 1. ROW LEVEL SECURITY ON EVERY TABLE. 0026 enabled it on the tables of the
--    day, and every later migration was supposed to repeat it for its own
--    tables. 0046 blog_posts, 0047 plugin_connect_requests and 0048
--    blog_categories did not, so those three were readable and writable by
--    any role holding table privileges: drafts, and the WordPress connect
--    handshake (approvals, code hashes, issued key ids). The statements
--    below were generated by drizzle-kit from the schema, which now declares
--    RLS on every table (src/lib/db/table.ts); on a table that already has
--    it they are no-ops. As in 0026: ENABLE, not FORCE, and NO policies -
--    the application connects as the table owner, which carries BYPASSRLS,
--    and nothing else should read these tables.
--
-- 2. NO TABLE, SEQUENCE OR FUNCTION PRIVILEGES FOR SUPABASE'S API ROLES.
--    Supabase's default privileges give `anon` and `authenticated` every
--    privilege on each new table, sequence and function in "public", and
--    every function is executable by PUBLIC. The application never uses the
--    Data API for these tables (it talks to Postgres directly, as the owner),
--    so all of that is accidental access. RLS stops row reads and writes,
--    but not TRUNCATE, and it depends on nobody ever adding a permissive
--    policy. This revokes those privileges on everything that exists, and
--    removes them from the default privileges of the role running the
--    migrations, so tables created by later migrations start without them.
--    Untouched on purpose:
--      - `service_role`: a server-side role that bypasses RLS anyway; Storage
--        (src/lib/images/storage.ts) authenticates with it.
--      - USAGE on schema "public": without object privileges it grants
--        nothing, and Supabase's own tooling expects it.
--      - pgvector's functions, which live in "public" on this database: they
--        belong to the extension and are filtered out below.
--      - default privileges of `supabase_admin`: only that role can change
--        them. Objects it creates in "public" are caught by the CI check
--        (src/lib/db/rls.postgres.test.ts) and by scripts/check-db-security.mjs.
--    On a database without these roles (plain Postgres, PGlite in tests) the
--    role-specific part does nothing.
--
-- Reversible, if ever needed:
--   GRANT ... ON <table> TO anon / authenticated, ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES
--   IN SCHEMA public GRANT ... TO ..., GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION ... TO PUBLIC,
--   ALTER TABLE <table> DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY.
-- Apply with the migration runner like any other migration; the application
-- keeps working throughout (it connects as the owner, with BYPASSRLS).
ALTER TABLE "addon_purchases" ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;--> statement-breakpoint
